tion":"Which two standard matrices are always idempotent?","options":["Every singular matrix and every symmetric matrix.","The zero square matrix and the identity matrix.","Every invertible matrix and every permutation matrix.","Every diagonal matrix and every triangular matrix."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2e17336ce67609b1f9f96e2a","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch19_idempotent_matrix_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If an idempotent matrix is invertible, what must it be?","options":["A nontrivial Jordan block.","The zero matrix.","Any diagonal matrix.","The identity matrix."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3a2c689756522f6be25a80b0","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch19_idempotent_matrix_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Is $\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$ idempotent?","options":["Yes, because it is diagonal.","No, because diagonal matrices cannot be idempotent.","Yes, because it is singular.","No, because its square is $\\begin{pmatrix}4&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ab3db779bab5231172666241","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch19_idempotent_matrix_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $P^2=P$, what is $P^3$?","options":["$I$","$0$","$P^2-P$","$P$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ff765869f883013cc3374ee5","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch19_idempotent_matrix_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Can an idempotent matrix be singular?","options":["Only if it is also the identity.","Yes. The zero matrix and nontrivial projections are singular examples.","No. Singular matrices cannot be squared.","No. Idempotent matrices are invertible, since $A^2=A$ can be divided by $A$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bb7e53c9a2fd2a418ce72ec0","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch19_idempotent_matrix_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What eigenvalues can an idempotent matrix have?","options":["Only -1 or 1.","Only 0 or 1.","Only positive integers.","Every real number."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0adbf065da20bb747a92e324","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch19_idempotent_matrix_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$. Is A idempotent?","options":["Only if the off-diagonal entry is 0.","No, because its square is zero.","Yes, because $A^2=A$.","No, because it is not diagonal."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e5bf19add06bc6686a3f2cba","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch19_idempotent_matrix_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $P^2=P$, what is $(I-P)^2$?","options":["$I-P$","$0$","$I+P$","$P$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5f6c0d27f4412e413239ddef","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch19_idempotent_matrix_007_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"$\\boldsymbol{P}_1=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\boldsymbol{P}_2=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$ are both idempotent, and both have the $x$-axis as range. Which statement is correct?","options":["Only $\\boldsymbol{P}_1$ is an orthogonal projection: only it is symmetric.","Both are orthogonal projections, because both satisfy $\\boldsymbol{P}^2=\\boldsymbol{P}$.","Neither is a projection matrix, because both have determinant $0$.","They are the same projection, because their ranges are equal."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7781a9a80adbc794bd546afa","time_limit":35},{"id":"la_batch19_idempotent_matrix_017_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{P}$ be an idempotent $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\boldsymbol{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$. Which statement about $\\boldsymbol{v}-\\boldsymbol{P}\\boldsymbol{v}$ is always true?","options":["It lies in $\\ker\\boldsymbol{P}$.","It is orthogonal to $\\operatorname{range}\\boldsymbol{P}$.","It is the zero vector.","It lies in $\\operatorname{range}\\boldsymbol{P}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0cd4735c1172c9a1ec81ee9e","time_limit":40}]}}